## Completion and direct review

Implemented an isolated provider-neutral command lifecycle foundation with:
- immutable, versioned, JSON-round-trippable command envelopes;
- provider ports for transport, lifecycle state, execution, and time;
- conditional claims with opaque fencing tokens and renewable leases;
- progress, completion, and failure mutations fenced by the current token;
- acknowledgement only after accepted terminal state;
- active and terminal duplicate handling;
- executable Gherkin scenarios and focused unit tests.

Direct review required and verified revisions for transport serialization, generic terminal states, contract-correct stale mutation, rotated renewal tokens, and explicit progress validation.

Validation:
- python -m behave tests/command_worker/features → 6 scenarios and 29 steps passed
- python -m pytest -q tests/command_worker → 30 passed
- python -m pytest -q plexus/cli/shared/command_dispatch_test.py plexus/cli/shared/command_tasks_test.py → 17 passed
- ruff check plexus/command_worker tests/command_worker → passed
- ruff format --check plexus/command_worker tests/command_worker → passed
- provider import boundary check → no forbidden imports

No existing Celery, dashboard, infrastructure, dependency, or lock files were changed. AWS adapters, heartbeat scheduling, cancellation, draining, and production executor integration remain follow-up work.
